  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Strong guide-safe fit: it separates embodiment labels from durable identity claims, requires a durable signed continuity artifact for key rotation, and gives Bob and Carol peer-local evidence to audit later. It stops short of a full portable browser/helper identity recipe and explicit XSS/storage guidance.",
    "strengths": [
      "Embodiment labels are explicitly non-authoritative, helping resist display-name and local-username confusion.",
      "Durable citation rules directly support auditing old-to-new key continuity across rotation.",
      "Scoped manifests and peer-local ledgers align well with sparse knowledge, no central registry, and long-term auditability."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "The pattern centers app-contract identity more clearly than actor-level signing-key identity.",
      "It does not specify the minimum continuity artifact or handshake shape for a v0 portable key recipe.",
      "Browser storage constraints and XSS caveats are only implied, not directly guided."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Implementers may conflate shared app identity with proof that two embodiments are the same signer.",
      "Without a concrete continuity artifact format, different clients may rotate keys incompatibly.",
      "Security claims around WebCrypto or IndexedDB persistence may be overstated unless compromise limits are explicit."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What exact durable object should link old and new signing keys across browser and plugin-host embodiments?",
      "How should actor identity be represented separately from app contract identity in the manifest and ledger?",
      "What minimum guide language is needed for browser key storage, exportability, and XSS risk?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  },
